.TH EUCLID-CLI-ETS 1 "August 2026" "euclid-cli 1.18.37" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ets \- manage Euclid FTP and SFTP transfer servers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
[\fB\-\-loglevel\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
.B ets
.I action
[\fIaction-options\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B ets
module talks to the Euclid gateway's ets service to define FTP and SFTP
endpoints that front an ESM bucket, and to start and stop them.
.PP
A transfer server ties three modules together. Clients authenticate
with their
.BR eam (1)
credentials, and are admitted only if the server names them directly or
they belong to one of its permitted user groups. Whatever they upload is
stored as an object in the server's
.BR esm (1)
bucket, and every listing and download reads back out of that bucket -
so the bucket, not any local directory, is what a client actually sees.
Two servers pointed at the same bucket therefore show the same contents,
as does a file put there by any other route.
.PP
.B ets
itself never speaks FTP or SFTP. It owns the server
.I definitions
only, and
.B start-server
and
.B stop-server
do nothing but record the state a server
.I should
be in. The Euclid manager notices within a few seconds and spawns or
stops the actual
.BR euclid-ftp (8)
or
.BR euclid-sftp (8)
process. This is why every action returns immediately, and why
.B list-server
output carries both a
.I desiredState
and an observed
.IR state :
the two differ while a server is coming up or going down, and stay
different if it cannot start at all.
.PP
Because the definitions are stored rather than sent as commands, a
manager restart brings every server marked running back by itself, and
.B ets
being unreachable at the moment a change is requested does not lose it.
.PP
All
.B ets
commands require administrator privileges; see
.B AUTHENTICATION
below.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B create-server
Defines a new FTP or SFTP server for a bucket. The server is created
stopped. See
.BR euclid-cli-ets-create-server (1).
.TP
.B update-server
Changes an existing server's settings; only the options given are
altered. See
.BR euclid-cli-ets-update-server (1).
.TP
.B list-servers
Lists the defined servers with their desired and observed state. See
.BR euclid-cli-ets-list-servers (1).
.TP
.B get-server
Shows one server's full definition. See
.BR euclid-cli-ets-get-server (1).
.TP
.B delete-server
Deletes a server definition, stopping it if it is running. See
.BR euclid-cli-ets-delete-server (1).
.TP
.B start-server
Marks a server as one that should be running. See
.BR euclid-cli-ets-start-server (1).
.TP
.B stop-server
Marks a server as one that should not be running, keeping its
definition. See
.BR euclid-cli-ets-stop-server (1).
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These options are given to
.B euclid-cli
itself, before the
.I ets
module name.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-endpoint " " \fIURL\fR
Euclid gateway endpoint to talk to, e.g. \fIhttps://localhost:5566\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-ca\-cert " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to a PEM CA certificate to trust in addition to the system trust
store, e.g. for self-signed development certificates.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pretty " " \fIBOOL\fR
Pretty-print the JSON response. Defaults to \fItrue\fR.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-loglevel " " \fILEVEL\fR
Log level: one of \fItrace\fR, \fIdebug\fR, \fIinfo\fR, \fIwarning\fR,
\fIerror\fR, \fIfatal\fR. Defaults to \fIinfo\fR.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print top-level usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the euclid-cli version and exit.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
.B ets
commands are authenticated using the session created by
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1),
loaded from
.IR $HOME/.euclid/credentials .
.PP
Every action is administrator-only, since each one changes or reveals
which network listeners exist and who can reach which bucket through
them. The CLI checks this before sending a request, to fail with a
clearer message, but the server checks independently on every request
and is the actual boundary.
.PP
Requesting help for an action does not require a session, so
.B "euclid-cli ets <action> \-\-help"
works before logging in.
.PP
If the server rejects a request with
.IR "HTTP 401" ,
run
.B euclid-cli eam login
again.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The action completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Argument parsing failed, the caller is not an authenticated
administrator, the request could not be sent, or the server rejected
it. Details are printed to standard error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/.euclid/credentials
JSON file holding the current session's bearer token and/or SigV4
access key, created by
.B euclid-cli eam login
and read by every
.B ets
action.
.SH EXAMPLES
Define an SFTP server for the \fIdropbox\fR bucket, open it to the
members of one user group, start it, and check that it came up:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 ets create-server \e
    --server-id sftp1 --protocol SFTP --port 2222 \e
    --bucket dropbox --groups transfer-users

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 ets start-server \e
    --server-id sftp1

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 ets list-servers
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets-create-server (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets-update-server (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets-list-servers (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets-get-server (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets-delete-server (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets-start-server (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets-stop-server (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1)
